<TeXmacs|2.1.4>

<style|<tuple|tmdoc|english>>

<\body>
  <tmdoc-title|Giving your document a name>

  We recommend you to give your document a name before you start to edit
  it, by saving it under a new name. This is achieved by selecting
  <menu|File|Save as>: click on the <menu|File> menu, and then on the
  <menu|Save as> entry. Alternatively, you may use the keyboard shortcut
  <shortcut|(choose-file save-buffer-as "Save TeXmacs file" "texmacs")>,
  which is also displayed next to the entry in the menu.

  A <em|file chooser> then appears. It allows you to browse the folders of
  your computer, using the keyboard or the mouse, and to choose the folder
  where your document will be saved. Its appearance depends on your
  system.

  In order to finish, type the name of your document, say
  <verbatim|test.tm>, in the field for the name of the file and confirm by
  pressing <key|return> or by clicking on the <menu|Save> button. You should
  always let the name of your file end with the <verbatim|.tm> <em|suffix>,
  so that anyone (and any program) can recognize that the file is a
  <TeXmacs> document.

  After saving, you will notice that the title bar of the window shows the
  new name <verbatim|test.tm> of your document instead of <verbatim|No name
  [1]>, and a message on the status bar confirms that the document has been
  saved.

